## 2. Scope

### In

- A terrain node: rectangular footprint, base elevation, width/depth, palette color and sample resolution; generated from a source group.
- Existing volumes as modifiers, starting with boxes and cylinders, then free-forms and smooth ramps. Raise, Lower and Set, with Fade in meters.
- Evaluation in outliner order, including nested groups. Existing reorder commands and undo drive it.
- Visible source geometry in the editor and a local Show modifiers toggle. Clean captures show the generated result.
- A shared deterministic heightfield evaluator for the browser and server; editable scene parameters stay the source of truth.
- Ordinary selection, source editing, bounds, move, copy, scale, mirror, persistence, undo and MCP support within the transform limits below.
- Native Unity Terrain export from the first increment. Later increments finish refresh, claims/content drift and a GLB mesh export.
- Experiments, measured performance and browser/Unity acceptance.

### Out (deliberately deferred)

- **Walking on terrain**, drawing/standing on its surface, `on` arrays and terrain-aware sight, clearance, slice, enclosure and lint checks. Track the integration work in §10; do not let it delay terrain authoring/export.
- **Live evaluation in Unity.** Unity imports evaluated heights. No source modifiers, runtime generator or second implementation of the terrain algorithm there.
- Terrain holes, caves, overhangs, bridges as heightfields, seamless tile stitching and infinite terrain. Ordinary meshes still cover bridges and overhangs.
- Painting, splat maps, biomes, erosion, noise, water, brush sculpting and procedural vegetation.
- Instances and arrays as terrain modifiers, terrains inside entity definitions, and nested/dependent terrains. These can extend the established evaluator later.
- Terrain yaw, pitch or roll. Source yaw is allowed; source tilt is deferred. No new GPU pipeline as a prerequisite.

