## 5. Supported geometry and shared evaluation

### Shapes

16.1 supports untilted box and cylinder volumes, including taper and bevel, so the experiment can compare actual hills. Reuse existing geometry to find the upper surface; do not replace a cone or rounded top with constant `y + height`.

16.2 adds concave/curved free-form volumes and smooth ramps. Ramps use their upper surface and width; stepped ramps and tilted sources are rejected for now. Rooms and holes are unsupported as modifiers, with a clear validation error rather than an accidental roof or CSG effect. Notes and lines may annotate the source group but never contribute heights. Instances/arrays cannot be modifiers in this phase. Unsupported additions/conversions into an owned source group fail atomically with an explanation.

Modifier shapes are authoring inputs, not ordinary exported solids. Their terrain evaluation is not changed by scene hole scope, and they do not cut/export neighboring geometry. A cave or cutout remains separate mesh work.

### Implementation

- Add a shared pure evaluator (e.g. `src/shared/terrain.ts`) with source collection, height sampling and mesh generation separated. Rendering and server export use the same canonical grid.
- Begin at 129 or 257 samples per side in the first increment; choose and record the default after measuring. Expose 129, 257, 513 and 1025 only as they meet the measured limits. Display cell spacing in meters in both axes: a 256 m square at 257 samples has 1 m cells.
- Cache by geometry, operations, fades, hierarchy order, relevant hidden state, terrain rectangle/base and resolution. Color changes should not resample heights.
- Start with bounded CPU work: evaluate only a modifier's projected bounds plus Fade; cache/rasterize source upper surfaces where useful. Avoid every sample testing every scene triangle.
- Add worker evaluation, coarse drag previews and finer-grained invalidation only when measurements justify them. These are not prerequisites for the tiny first editor. Any asynchronous result must carry its revision so an old drag cannot overwrite a newer result.
- Captures and exports use the canonical result for the requested revision, never an unfinished/coarse preview. Export remains possible without an editor tab.
- Picking and bounds use generated terrain geometry. Keep it outside the current watertight closed-shape CSG path.

Measure 20 and 100 modifiers at supported resolutions: rebuild time, drag responsiveness, memory and export time. Record the machine and scene sizes. Establish practical limits from those results instead of claiming unmeasured performance.

