## 6. Editor and transforms

The Terrain tool draws its rectangle like a box footprint, with the familiar snap and center-drawing conventions. 16.1 may use a simple creation button and inspector dimensions instead of a finished drawing tool. The inspector shows size, base elevation, resolution, sample spacing and color. Selecting a source shows Raise/Lower/Set and Fade alongside its existing geometry controls.

Contributing shapes stay visible, recognizable and selectable in orlablocks. Distinguish them as authoring geometry (outline/translucent overlay as needed to avoid z-fighting); do not secretly set `hidden`. A local **Show modifiers** toggle reveals the generated ground alone. Clean captures, shots and `render_view` omit modifier overlays. The outliner still lists the shapes and is the operation stack.

Moving the assembly moves both ground and inputs. Moving a terrain alone moves its sampling rectangle and base; it does not move its source group. Resizing the rectangle changes coverage, not the shapes. Moving a modifier changes only its contribution. Scaling an assembly scales source geometry, base/positions about the chosen pivot, dimensions and Fade; sample count stays fixed. Mirroring an assembly mirrors its sources and rectangle. Reject transforms that would yaw or tilt a terrain, explaining the restriction before committing any partial edit.

Copying a complete assembly remaps its source reference and preserves outliner order. Copying a bare terrain also clones its source subtree and places it beside the copy; it must never unexpectedly share editable inputs. Clipboard operations use the same dependency rule. Copying a modifier alone follows normal destination-group behavior. Deleting a terrain leaves the source shapes as ordinary geometry; deleting its source group clears the reference and leaves a flat terrain. Undo restores both. Ungrouping the owned source root clears its binding in the same step; its released shapes become ordinary geometry. Nested grouping/ungrouping preserves flattened traversal order unless the user explicitly moves rows.

