## 8. Unity export and refresh

### First import in 16.1

Extend the versioned manifest and atomic export payload with a terrain record: ID, rectangle, sample count, vertical origin/range, heights and revision. Write the data before the manifest using the existing atomic pattern. Update both producer and Unity reader with explicit format compatibility checks; do not reinterpret a new payload as the old format.

The exporter evaluates the terrain in TypeScript. Unity creates a `TerrainData` asset, a Terrain and a TerrainCollider. Source shapes and their authoring-only group subtree do not become mesh objects, colliders or entity dependencies in the export. A source-only group containing annotations is omitted as well. Ordinary nodes outside it keep existing behavior.

Use a basic URP Terrain-compatible material/layer with the chosen palette color. Ordinary mesh materials are not automatically Terrain materials. Respect `#no-collisions` by omitting the TerrainCollider.

Unity heights are normalized 0..1, indexed [row,column]. Its supported square heightmap resolutions include 129, 257, 513 and 1025; physical width and depth may differ. Preserve negative world elevations with the exported vertical origin/range, and use a nonzero range for a flat terrain. Reverse sample rows for the existing z flip and place the Terrain origin at the appropriate minimum corner. Terrain output remains axis-aligned with unit transform scale; dimensions are baked into TerrainData.

Test with an asymmetric fixture (different heights at each corner), a rectangular footprint, a nonzero position/base and a negative depression. Compare canonical grid samples with imported data, then inspect Unity rendering and collider heights at representative points. Native Terrain LOD/triangulation can differ from the browser mesh; establish a measured tolerance rather than promising identical triangles.

16.1 includes a manual second export/sync updating the same terrain. It does not need a live link, Auto sync polish, GLB or the full material mapping workflow to prove the loop. Existing claims must still be honored from the first importer; document the early test's Claim-before-sculpting limitation until content drift detection lands.

### Finished sync in 16.4

Keep a stable TerrainData asset per scene terrain. Revision includes the evaluated content/settings, so reorder or a source edit updates only affected terrain records. Do not mutate a claimed terrain's asset through a cache shared with another terrain. Respect existing transform drift, deletion/orphans, Auto sync and Unity undo, including undo of asset changes.

Add heightmap-content drift detection before overwriting locally sculpted TerrainData. Report the conflict with Revert/Claim choices, analogous to transform drift. This does not import Unity edits back into orlablocks. Define the managed fields explicitly (heights, size, generated material/layer and collider state); preserve unrelated Unity additions. Test a claimed asset and a locally sculpted unclaimed one on both changed and unchanged exports.

GLB exports the evaluated terrain mesh, omitting source geometry. Terrain nodes do not participate in Claim as ProBuilder in this phase. Source removal must be distinguished from ordinary export deletion when existing scene shapes become terrain inputs: preserve user additions under those objects using the established orphan rules.

